  "role": "assistant",
  "content": "## CPU Scheduling\n\nCPU scheduling is how an operating system decides which ready process gets the CPU next, so that the processor stays busy and every process makes fair progress.\n\n### How it works\n\n1. Processes that are ready to run wait in the ready queue.\n2. The scheduler picks one according to its policy (FCFS, SJF, Priority, Round Robin).\n3. The dispatcher performs a context switch and gives that process the CPU.\n4. The process runs until it finishes, blocks on I/O, or its time quantum expires.\n5. The scheduler picks the next process and the cycle repeats.\n\n### Key points\n\n- Goals: high CPU utilisation, high throughput, low waiting and turnaround time, fairness.\n- Preemptive policies (Round Robin, SRTF) can interrupt a running process; non-preemptive ones cannot.\n- A very small time quantum causes excessive context-switch overhead; a very large one degrades to FCFS.\n\n---\n*Offline fallback: no language model is connected, so this answer came from StudyMate's small built-in knowledge base. Start Ollama (`ollama serve` + `ollama pull <model>`) for full answers.*",
